{
  "configuration_id": "config_025",
  "reason": "The current best (config_007, acc=0.725) uses scvi_n200 + imagenet_vit_l + multiprofile + transformer + weighted. The multiprofile scvi_n200 + imagenet_vit_l 2×2 grid (aggregation × fusion) is nearly saturated (avgpool+weighted=0.706, transformer+weighted=0.725, transformer+concat=0.721), leaving only avgpool+concat (config_008) which is very unlikely to beat 0.725. The most uncertain remaining direction is the single-profile architecture with the best encoders: it removes the aggregation step entirely and fuses a single RNA profile with the image. config_025 (scvi_n200 + imagenet_vit_l + singleprofile + weighted) tests whether bypassing multiprofile aggregation could yield comparable or better accuracy, since weighted fusion has been the slightly stronger choice. This is the best use of experiment 8 because: (1) it reduces uncertainty about a major architectural axis (single vs. multiprofile) that is completely unexplored with the top encoders, (2) if it performs competitively it opens a cheaper path to high accuracy, and (3) with only 2 experiments remaining, resolving this binary question informs the final experiment choice. Expected relative cost: lower than multiprofile transformer configs (~2–3 GPU hours vs. 3.6–4.8) since there is no aggregation step, making it a cost-efficient exploration."
}

